      "text": "Build a clean, simple, mobile-first web app called “Hebrew Reader” that reads Hebrew text aloud.\n\nPurpose:\nThe app lets a user paste Hebrew text, generate spoken audio, and control playback easily. It should work especially well on Android phones, including Samsung Galaxy devices, but also on desktop browsers.\n\nLayout:\n- Use a calm, uncluttered dashboard with a single main card centered on the page.\n- Support right-to-left (RTL) layout whenever the text language is Hebrew.\n- Use Hebrew-friendly typography, large readable text, high contrast, and accessible buttons.\n- The page should have a title: “Hebrew Text Reader”.\n- Add a short subtitle: “Paste Hebrew text and listen to it clearly.”\n\nInput area:\n- Include a large multiline text box with RTL text direction by default.\n- Placeholder text in Hebrew: “הדבק כאן טקסט בעברית…”\n- Include a Paste button that requests clipboard access and inserts copied text.\n- Include a Clear button that removes all text.\n- Show a character count beneath the text box.\n- Disable playback controls until there is text to read.\n\nText-to-speech:\n- Use a text-to-speech service or browser speech-synthesis fallback that supports Hebrew (Israel), language code he-IL.\n- Prefer a clear, natural-sounding Hebrew voice.\n- Let the user choose from available Hebrew voices when more than one is available.\n- Read the pasted text exactly as written. Do not translate, summarize, rewrite, or send the text to an AI model for interpretation.\n- Handle Hebrew punctuation and paragraphs naturally.\n- If no Hebrew voice is available, show a clear message explaining how to install or enable a Hebrew text-to-speech voice on the device.\n\nPlayback controls:\nAfter text is entered, show a playback card with:\n- Play button\n- Pause button\n- Resume button\n- Stop button\n- Rewind 10 seconds button\n- Forward 30 seconds button\n- A visible progress bar with elapsed time and estimated total duration\n- Playback speed selector: 0.75x, 1x, 1.25x, 1.5x, and 2x\n- Voice selector\n- A volume control that uses the device/browser audio volume when permitted\n\nBehavior:\n- Pressing Play begins reading from the start.\n- Pause pauses at the current point.\n- Resume continues from the paused point.\n- Stop returns playback to the beginning.\n- Rewind 10 seconds and Forward 30 seconds should move through the text/audio as accurately as the selected TTS method allows.\n- If exact seek controls are unavailable with browser speech synthesis, clearly indicate this in the interface and implement the closest practical behavior.\n- Preserve the pasted text and selected voice/speed during the current session.\n- Show useful status text, such as “Ready,” “Reading,” “Paused,” “Finished,” or an error message.\n\nAccessibility and privacy:\n- All controls must have clear accessible labels for screen readers.\n- Make buttons large enough for touch use.\n- Support keyboard navigation.\n- Do not permanently store pasted text unless the user explicitly chooses to save it.\n- Do not require account sign-in for the basic paste-and-listen experience.\n\nDesign:\n- Keep the interface simple and polished, with no chat interface, no unnecessary menus, and no advertisements.\n- Use a responsive card-based design with subtle borders, rounded corners, and a light background.\n- Include light and dark mode support.\n\nArchitecture decision:\nChoose the simplest privacy-conscious architecture that meets the requirements.\nFirst, briefly explain the architecture you selected and why before implementing it.\nPrefer browser/device text-to-speech for a first version if it supports a clear Hebrew voice and the requested controls.\nOnly add a server-side TTS API if it is necessary to support reliable Hebrew speech or a real seekable audio player with accurate progress, rewind, and forward controls.\nDo not add login, a database, or permanent storage for the first version.\nKeep credentials and API keys server-side only; never expose them in browser code.",
