      "text": "**Developing the Hebrew Reader**\n\nI\u0027m focusing now on the essential features of this Hebrew Reader web app. Mobile-first design is paramount for Android/Samsung compatibility. I\u0027m prioritizing RTL support, and a clean interface. Hebrew TTS is a must. The primary buttons are shaping up: paste/clear, character count, voice selection, speed adjustment, and basic playback controls.\n\n\n**Evaluating TTS Options**\n\nI\u0027ve been weighing the pros and cons of implementing text-to-speech with either the browser\u0027s Web Speech API or a server-side API. The browser API offers privacy and zero cost, but its seek functionality is limited. Server-side APIs provide high-quality voices and easy seeking via an audio file, but they introduce cost, latency, and dependency on external services. The guiding principle remains a privacy-focused and simplest architecture, so the browser API is preferred for now unless Hebrew voices prove unreliable, or seek functionality becomes a necessity.\n\n\n**Finalizing the API Choice**\n\nI\u0027ve decided, finally, on the Web Speech API for this Hebrew reader. Despite the seeking limitations, the privacy and simplicity are too compelling to ignore, particularly for a first version. I\u0027ll simulate seek functionality, even if it\u0027s an approximation. The structure will be a single HTML file. Tailwind CSS via CDN will handle the responsive UI, including RTL support. I\u0027m focusing on the logic now: checking for `speechSynthesis`, filtering Hebrew voices (`he-IL`), managing the reader\u0027s state (text, playback status, index), and working out the seeking logic with `onboundary`.\n\n\n",
